      <!-- The Golden Rule: The JR Yamanote Line -->
      <h2>1. The Golden Rule of Tokyo Geography: The Yamanote Loop</h2>

      <p>
        Before you book a single hotel room in Tokyo, understand this foundational rule: <strong>always stay within a 5-to-8 minute walk of a JR Yamanote Line station</strong>.
      </p>

      <p>
        The <strong>JR Yamanote Line</strong> (identifiable on maps by its bright green color) is an elevated circular railway loop connecting all of Tokyo’s primary entertainment, cultural, and commercial hubs: Shinjuku, Shibuya, Harajuku, Ikebukuro, Ueno, Akihabara, and Tokyo Station. Trains arrive every 2 to 3 minutes throughout the day.
      </p>

      <div class="roam-callout-ai">
        <strong>💡 Pro Transit Tip:</strong> Tokyo has two distinct subway systems (Tokyo Metro and Toei Subway) plus several private rail operators (JR East, Keio, Odakyu). When you stay on the Yamanote Loop, you bypass confusing inter-line ticket gates and can reach 80% of Tokyo's iconic sights with zero transfers.
      </div>

      <!-- Head-to-Head: Shinjuku vs Shibuya -->
      <h2>2. Head-to-Head: Shinjuku vs. Shibuya for First-Timers</h2>

      <p>
        The most common dilemma for first-time visitors is choosing between Tokyo’s two western giants: <strong>Shinjuku</strong> and <strong>Shibuya</strong>. They are just 3 train stops (approx. 7 minutes) apart on the Yamanote Line, yet they offer distinctly different atmospheres.
      </p>

