      <!-- FAQ Section -->
      <h2>4. Frequently Asked Questions: Where to Stay in Tokyo</h2>

      <div class="faq-accordion">
        <div class="faq-item active">
          <h4 class="faq-question">Where is the absolute best area to stay in Tokyo for first-timers?</h4>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>
              For first-time visitors, <strong>Shinjuku</strong> is widely considered the #1 all-around base. It sits directly on the JR Yamanote Loop line, connects to Narita and Haneda via direct express trains, offers thousands of izakayas and ramen alleys (Omoide Yokocho, Golden Gai), and keeps you within a 15-minute train ride of Shibuya, Harajuku, and Roppongi.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h4 class="faq-question">Is it better to stay in Shinjuku or Shibuya?</h4>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>
              Choose <strong>Shinjuku</strong> if you prioritize hyper-convenient day trips (Hakone, Mount Fuji, Kamakura), expansive skyscraper hotels, and lively nightlife. Choose <strong>Shibuya</strong> if you prefer trendsetting youth fashion, creative boutique cafés, walkable side-streets (Cat Street, Daikanyama), and a vibrant pedestrian energy centered around Shibuya Crossing.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h4 class="faq-question">How many days should you spend in Tokyo for a first visit?</h4>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>
              We recommend a minimum of <strong>5 to 7 full days in Tokyo</strong>. This allows you to explore the major distinct hubs—West Tokyo (Shinjuku, Shibuya, Harajuku), East Tokyo (Asakusa, Akihabara, Ueno), Central Tokyo (Ginza, Imperial Palace, Tokyo Station), and Tsukiji/Toyosu fish markets—without feeling overwhelmed or rushed.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h4 class="faq-question">Should I fly into Haneda Airport (HND) or Narita Airport (NRT)?</h4>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>
              <strong>Haneda (HND)</strong> is significantly closer and more convenient, situated just 25–35 minutes by Tokyo Monorail or Keikyu line into central Tokyo (approx. ¥500–¥650). Narita (NRT) is located 65 km east of Tokyo in Chiba Prefecture; the Narita Express (N'EX) or Skyliner takes 45–60 minutes and costs ¥2,500–¥3,200. Always pick Haneda if airfares are comparable.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h4 class="faq-question">Do I need a pocket WiFi or an eSIM for Tokyo in 2026?</h4>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>
              In 2026, travel eSIMs like <strong>Airalo's Moshi Moshi Japan plan</strong> have almost entirely replaced bulky pocket WiFi routers. An eSIM installs directly to your phone via QR code before departure, provides immediate 4G/5G data upon landing at Haneda or Narita, eliminates the hassle of carrying and recharging extra hardware, and costs as little as $4.50 to $18 USD.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

